+# S48 Review-AI audit — work order and adjudication (2026-09-24)
+
+The Review AI audited the merged S47 series (`10c668fd`: andn-gated CH
+mux fold, earliest-And majority, acc-resident ALU consumption) without a
+toolchain. Adjudication below is mine; every accepted finding was
+implemented in this session (P0→P2 work order), and the battery — not
+the audit — is the authority on whether it holds.
+
+## Adjudicated findings and their disposition
+
+| Finding | Verdict | Disposition |
+|---|---|---|
+| F1 — 422 backend lines (`machinst_window_rax_free`, `machinst_window_defs`) with zero backend tests | **Agree — the strongest finding.** Verification gaps I should have closed myself; the predicates guard the highest-stakes silent rewrite in the MachInst path. | P0-1: 5 field-accurate tests constructing real `MachInst` values (implicit-rax kinds, every explicit-rax operand position, the clean window, per-kind def collection, the rotated-latch near-miss). Deleting or widening any match arm of either predicate fails at least one. |
+| F2 — the majority rank branch untested | **Agree, with a correction the audit missed:** my first "decline" fixture was itself wrong. One FOREIGN And is soundly *keepable* (kept terms are read-only; only repurposed producers must stay in-block — the v1 soundness rule). The decline needs TWO foreign arms (every role assignment then requires repurposing a foreign producer). | P0-3: three tests — single-foreign KEPT (fires, reads the foreign And directly), two-foreign (declines), all-local (earliest kept wins). |
+| F3 — andn-fusion criteria in three spellings (fold prediction, backend detector, emitter) that could drift | **Agree on the mechanism; the emitter site already had its own ISA gate, so the real drift risk was the middle-end/backend pair.** | P0-2: extracted `andn_fusion_predicted_at`; `detect_and_not_fusions` made `pub(crate)`; a contract test runs BOTH REAL predicates over ten constructed scenarios and asserts agreement. P0-4: the width domain single-sourced as `alu_fusion_width_ok`. |
+| F4 — the nbody/rbtree gate's positive control was removed, leaving the wiring pin only | **Partially push back.** The S47 removal was an honest decision with the obsoletion data recorded in three places; shape-pinning a measured regression is how you pin a bug as a contract. But the audit is right that pinning the knob's *liveness* completes the protection without pinning any shape. | F5: `check_ivsr_scalar_derived_default.sh` — runtime differentials both ways PLUS the default pin (knob-set and knob-unset asm must differ: catches both a silent default flip and a rotted no-op knob). |
+| F6 — the andn census grep was whole-file | **Agree.** Compensating changes across functions could pass a whole-file count. | Region-scoped census: the function is extracted by boundary markers first; missing markers fail with their own message. |
+| F9 — unguarded `next()` / silent re-scoping in the gate's python | **Agree.** A StopIteration traceback reads as a gate bug, not a shape drift; the `except StopIteration: f0, f1 = 0, len(raw)` fallback silently re-scoped check D to the whole file. | Distinct shape-drift exits: every marker miss fails with its own "shape drift" message; the silent fallback is gone. |
+| F7 — acc-resident substitution lacked a register-class guard | **Agree — cheap hardening.** The acc cache is GPR-only by construction today, but a fail-closed guard makes the invariant explicit instead of emergent. | `!state.vector_values.contains(id)` added to the substitution admission (covers the 128-bit class: every vector128 member is a vector member). |
+| F10/F13 — `check_nbody_rbtree_perf_shapes.sh` name lies (rbtree content removed) | **Agree.** A gate whose name lies trains people to ignore gate names. | Renamed `check_nbody_load_cse_shapes.sh`; header documents the removal and points at the knob's own gate; CI + ci_local re-wired. |
+| F11 — the two codegen-changing knobs undocumented in the registry | **Agree.** `CCC_IVSR_SCALAR_DERIVED` was reachable only from source comments. | Focused registry section in `docs/optimization-passes.md` (measured defaults, why, and the gate that pins each). |
+| F12 — `has_scalar_andn` duplicated across the two bit_idioms tiers | **Agree.** A future edit to one copy would desynchronize fold-time from emission-time. | Single `resolved_has_scalar_andn` spelling; both tiers call it. |
+| F14 — provenance note missing for the campaign's measurements | **Agree — this file and PERF-PROVENANCE-S45-S48.md close it.** | Written. |
+
+## Findings I verified but did not act on
+
+- The audit's "cleared, not buried" items (the multi_def read in the
+  majority fold, the no-fail-open catch-all in `machinst_window_defs`)
+  were re-verified by reading: both are load-bearing as written.
+- The audit could not run anything. Its static claims were all confirmed
+  by the battery after implementation; where its model of the code was
+  wrong (F2's single-foreign decline expectation), the correction is
+  documented above rather than silently absorbed.
+
+## Battery status after the work order
+
+`cargo test` (incl. the 5 new MachInst window tests, the 10-scenario
+contract test, the 3 multi-block majority tests) green;
+`ci_local.sh --fast` green including the two new/renamed gates;
+chacha20 measurements unchanged (this work adds tests and guards, not
+codegen paths — the only codegen-visible change is the F7 guard, which
